# `core.autocrlf` is true on this bench. `-text` tells git to store
# docs/HANDOFF.md's bytes as they are, so an edit stages as the lines that
# changed rather than as a whole-file rewrite -- 12,000 lines changed to show
# 60 -- which would bury the actual change and break `git log` as the incident
# record for the one file that most depends on being readable that way.
#
# **The blob is LF, and the guard cuts both ways**, corrected 03 Sep. This note
# used to say the blob was CRLF; it had drifted to LF at some point, and `-text`
# was faithfully preserving that. A patch script using `Path.write_text` then
# converted the working copy to CRLF on the way past -- Windows translates `\n`
# on write -- and the file staged as 25,053 changed lines to show a paragraph.
# Reverted in the commit after.
#
# So: when scripting an edit to this file, write bytes or pass `newline="\n"`.
# Whichever ending it holds, the rule is that a commit must not change it as a
# side effect. Normalising deliberately is fine; doing it by accident costs the
# history.
docs/HANDOFF.md -text
